Probed in a cp -a copy at /tmp/claude-1000/.../scratchpad/z7check/budget2. Real
tree untouched.

1. Non-bumping writers (grep for every saveInternal/bumpLocked call site in
   settings.go): the ONLY save that skips bumpLocked is loadInternalContext's
   migration-normalize path (settings.go:674-683, sm.saveInternal without
   bump, doc'd at 866-869). Traced every path that can leave sm.cache nil
   (InvalidateCache:543, reconcileActiveScenarioLocked:590,
   BeginExternalRewrite end:619, SwitchScenario:1971, CreateScenario:2015,
   DeleteScenario:2089, RenameScenario:2142, save-error paths:789/843) —
   every one of them either bumps in the SAME locked critical section before
   the migration-write can run, or (save-error paths) leaves disk unchanged,
   so no external reader can ever observe a stale revision number paired
   with post-migration content. RenameScenario/DeleteScenario write directly
   via sm.store.WriteFile bypassing saveInternal but still bump
   unconditionally in the same lock — no gap. Checked restore.go (only other
   direct-rewrite site): correctly gated behind BeginExternalRewrite. Found
   a genuinely un-guarded sibling — ApplyOverrides (settings.go:1270, used
   by handlers_live.go and mcpsvc/plan/register.go) has a scenario-only
   guard with NO expectedRevision param, so a same-scenario concurrent edit
   could still race THAT write path — but this is a different feature
   (live-apply/MCP), never touched by Z7's file list, and Z7's task scope is
   explicitly the sync.go handleWhatIfSync/handleWhatIfSyncApply path.
   Reporting as a backlog finding, not a Z7 FAIL (scope rule, CLAUDE.md
   "recurring defect classes" / rule 5).

2. Revision reset / false-PASS on restart: sm.revision is only ever
   incremented (grep "\.revision\b" — single "sm.revision++" at
   bumpLocked, no reset/reassignment anywhere). The task's suggested
   cross-instance false-PASS (two SettingsManager instances racing) requires
   two live instances pointed at the same directory; grepped every
   NewSettingsManager call site in production code — exactly one
   (cmd/server/main.go:86), and the MCP tools are mounted in the same
   binary/process sharing that same retirementMgr (cmd/server/mcp_mount_test.go
   present, only one construction site). So the false-PASS scenario the
   attack angle describes cross-instance is not reachable in this codebase's
   actual topology; within one process the counter is provably monotonic.
   Documented and accepted as a known limitation at the revision field's doc
   comment ("monotonic within one process").

3. Apply-side load / ABA: handleWhatIfSyncApply loads `settings` via a
   plain, unlocked LoadContext (sync.go:442) after the guard-field parsing,
   then applySyncPlan mutates it, then saveAndRecalcIfScenario performs the
   locked compare-and-write. Verified the guard's correctness does NOT
   depend on what `settings` content was loaded — SaveWithRevisionIfScenario
   (settings.go:823-847) re-checks expectedRevision against sm.revision
   INSIDE the write lock immediately before the write, so any concurrent
   same-scenario save that bumps sm.revision between preview and this save
   is caught regardless of load timing. Proved the exploit is closed AND
   proved the test proves it: patched the revision check to `if false && ...`
   (simulating pre-fix behavior) — TestHandleWhatIfSyncApply_
   ConcurrentSameScenarioEditNotLost failed (200, wanted 409) as expected;
   reverted, it passes. Fail-end and pass-end both validated directly,
   not by trusting the test's name.

4. Parse edge cases: expected_revision via strconv.Atoi — negative values
   parse cleanly (no panic) and correctly mismatch sm.revision (>=0) →
   409, not a crash; out-of-int64-range values return ErrRange → 400;
   leading zeros parse fine as decimal. blank/missing/non-numeric all
   produce 400 with the retarget header — confirmed via
   TestHandleWhatIfSyncApply_MissingGuardFieldsRejected's three added
   sub-cases (blank/missing/non-numeric), all passing.

5. errors.As / message content: grepped every &ScenarioConflictError{...}
   construction site — all pointer literals, consistent with
   `errors.As(err, &conflictErr)` where conflictErr is *ScenarioConflictError
   in handlers.go:246/269. The two distinct guard failures produce distinct,
   accurate messages: scenario mismatch says "the active scenario is X...
   (the active scenario changed since the check)"; revision mismatch says
   "the plan changed since it was loaded (it is now at revision %d...)" —
   no cross-contamination/misleading text between the two failure modes.

6. Template/JSON parity: sync-preview.html has all three hidden fields
   (expected_scenario, plan_hash, expected_revision) sourced from the same
   map passed to RenderPartial; syncPreviewResponse struct has matching
   json:"expected_revision" tag; both branches in handleWhatIfSync
   (sync.go:352-367) read from the SAME `expectedRevision` local returned
   by LoadContextWithRevision — single source of truth, no divergent
   computation.

7. Commands run: `go build ./...` clean; `go vet
   ./internal/handlers/whatif/... ./internal/services/retirement/...` clean;
   `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... ./internal/services/retirement/...
   -count=1` all ok; `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/...
   ./internal/services/retirement/ -race -count=1` all ok (38.9s / 69.2s, no
   data races flagged despite the fix touching shared sm.revision/sm.cache
   under RWMutex); targeted -race -count=3/-count=5 on the new/changed tests
